We compute $\omega \wedge \omega$ where
$$\omega = dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1 + dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2.$$

Expanding the wedge product bilinearly:
$$\omega \wedge \omega = A_1 + A_2 + A_3 + A_4,$$
where
- $A_1 = (dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1) \wedge (dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1)$,
- $A_2 = (dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1) \wedge (dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2)$,
- $A_3 = (dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2) \wedge (dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1)$,
- $A_4 = (dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2) \wedge (dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2)$.

**Term $A_1$ and $A_4$ vanish.** In $A_1$, the 1-form $dz_1$ appears twice, so by antisymmetry of the wedge product $A_1 = 0$. Likewise in $A_4$ the 1-form $dz_2$ appears twice, so $A_4 = 0$.

**Relate $A_2$ and $A_3$.** We have
$$A_3 = dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2 \wedge dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1.$$
To reorder this into $dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1 \wedge dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2$, move $dz_1$ leftward past $dz_2$ and $d\bar{z}_2$ (two transpositions), then move $d\bar{z}_1$ leftward past $dz_2$ and $d\bar{z}_2$ (two more transpositions). The total number of transpositions is $4$, giving a sign $(-1)^4 = +1$. Hence
$$A_3 = +\, dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1 \wedge dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2 = A_2.$$

**Combine.** Therefore
$$\omega \wedge \omega = A_2 + A_3 = 2\, dz_1 \wedge d\bar{z}_1 \wedge dz_2 \wedge d\bar{z}_2.$$

